{
  "raw": {
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27377?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Limited Term Adjunct, Construction Engineering Mgmt., Civil & Architectural Engineering & Mechanics",
      "description": "Limited Term Adjunct, Construction Engineering Mgmt., Civil & Architectural Engineering & Mechanics | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Limited Term Adjunct, Construction Engineering Mgmt., Civil & Architectural Engineering & Mechanics Tucson, AZ, United States | req27377 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/15/2026 Back to Search Limited Term Adjunct, Construction Engineering Mgmt., Civil & Architectural Engineering & Mechanics Posting Number req27377 Department Civil Arch Engr and Mechanics Department Website Link caem.engineering.arizona.edu Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering and Mechanics invites qualified applicants to apply for a Limited Term Adjunct position. The incumbent will teach one of our construction classes such as CE 381 Construction Engineering Management, CE482 Construction Project Planning, Scheduling, and Control, CE485 Construction Equipment & Methods, or CE 497 a special topics construction course in the spring semester of academic year 2026-27. CE381 Construction Engineering Management provides an overview of the fundamentals of construction management such as project delivery methods, construction contracts, bid packages, takeoff and estimating, planning and scheduling, equipment ownership and operation, construction labor, cost control, and construction safety. CE482 Construction Project Planning, Scheduling, and Control provides an in depth look at scheduling concepts and implementation of scheduling principles with Oracle’s P6 software. Topics include bar charts, network diagrams, critical path method, precedence networks, resource allocation and leveling, schedule updating and project control, schedule compression and time-cost trade-off, schedule reports and memos, construction delays and claims, and schedule risk management. CE485 Construction Equipment & Methods provides an in-depth look at the capabilities of construction equipment, safety measures with construction equipment, methods for calculating productivity, and construction operations with multiple pieces of construction equipment. Topics include soil characteristics, fundamentals of earthmoving, dozers, loaders, scrapers, deep excavation and shoring, concrete and asphalt paving equipment, excavators, soil compactors, lifting and loading equipment, cranes, temporary structures, tunneling, and traffic control. CE497 Special Topics in Construction includes in-depth look at topics relevant to the construction industry. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Conduct three hours of class meetings per week. The days and times vary depending on the course, however, classes typically meet for 75 minutes twice per week, or 50 minutes three times per week. The course syllabus and material will be provided by the department head for CE 381, 482 or 485. Prepare and organize class\n       meetings such as content presentation, problem-solving, group learning,\n       guest presenters from industry, and site visits. Prepare and assign various\n       assignments (e.g., homework, quizzes, etc.) to complement and assess\n       team learning activities. Grade student work. When\n       the class size is large enough, a grader can be made available and the\n       instructor will provide guidance to the grader. Invite and coordinate with\n       industry guest presenters. Work with presenters to develop content and\n       student activities and assignments that that relate to the guest\n       presenter’s content. Hold physical or virtual\n       office hours weekly throughout the semester. Be available via email\n       throughout the week. Enter grades into the UA\n       online system. Maintain the course website\n       in Brightspace. Campus support is available to help with Brightspace. Minimum Qualifications BS in Civil Engineering, Construction Engineering, Construction Management or relevant discipline and a minimum of 10-yrs experience in the related course area. Extensive\n     experience in vertical and/or horizontal construction. The\n     willingness to integrate industry partners into the teaching of select\n     class topics. Demonstrate knowledge of industry software for project\nmanagement, estimating, scheduling, etc. Preferred Qualifications MS/MBA or PhD or PE with equivalent experience. Relevant certifications or licenses such as a P.E. license, CCM, PMP, CPC, contractors license, DBIA, Lean, Envision, OSHA certification, etc. Experience managing construction projects, field operations, estimating, scheduling, contracts, or project controls. Experience with projects of varying size and complexity. Experience with multiple project delivery methods. Leadership experience in the construction industry. Experience teaching at the university level with preference to having taught this or a similar course. A network of local construction professionals to assist with guest lectures and site visits and connection with construction organizations such as DBIA, AGC, ABA, etc. Rank Lecturer Tenure Information Adjunct (NTE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Part Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 8 Job FTE 0.2 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible No Benefits Rate of Pay $8,000 Compensation Type Per Course Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Dominic Boccelli, Department Head dboccelli@arizona.edu Open Date 10/15/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Resume and Cover Letter Special Instructions to Applicant Cover Letter indicating your background and experience as well as the course(s) you would be interested in teaching, and the names and contact information of three references. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27358?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Pathology and Laboratory Medicine (Career Track)",
      "description": "Lecturer, Pathology and Laboratory Medicine (Career Track) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Lecturer, Pathology and Laboratory Medicine (Career Track) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27358 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/15/2026 Back to Search Lecturer, Pathology and Laboratory Medicine (Career Track) Posting Number req27358 Department Pathology and Lab Medicine Department Website Link https://pathology.arizona.edu/ Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address 1501 N. Campbell Ave., Tucson, AZ 85719 USA Position Highlights The Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine at the University of Arizona College of Medicine – Tucson invites applications for an entry-level Lecturer faculty position. This position is ideal for individuals seeking to launch a career in academic medicine and health sciences education. The department seeks a motivated educator with training in pathology, laboratory medicine, histology, biomedical sciences, or a related field who is passionate about teaching and mentoring the next generation of healthcare professionals and scientists. The successful candidate will join a collaborative educational environment and receive mentorship from experienced faculty while developing expertise in teaching, curriculum development, learner assessment, and academic service. This position includes opportunities for training in evidence-based teaching and learner assessment and opportunities to participate in educational research and scholarly activities. Faculty appointed to this career-track, non-tenure-eligible position will contribute to the educational mission of the College of Medicine through classroom, laboratory, and small-group instruction. This position as a Lecturer in the Educator Series Track offers a structured pathway for professional growth and promotion based on demonstrated excellence in teaching, educational innovation, mentoring, and service. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; UA/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; state retirement plan; access to UA recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocation services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Deliver\n     high-quality instruction in Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine\n     courses; currently these include, but are not restricted to, PATH415/515\n     and medical student pre-clerkship education. Support\n     student learning through classroom, laboratory, and small-group teaching\n     activities. Participate\n     in course planning, assessment, and continuous improvement efforts. Provide\n     mentoring and academic support to students, preceptors, and trainees. Pursue\n     ongoing professional development in teaching and education. Demonstrated interest in teaching, mentoring, or educational activities. Excellent interpersonal, communication, and organizational skills. Commitment to working effectively with students, trainees, faculty, and staff from various backgrounds. Minimum Qualifications Master's\n     degree or higher in Pathology, Laboratory Medicine, Clinical Laboratory\n     Science, Biomedical Sciences, Anatomy, Cell Biology, Molecular Biology, or\n     a closely related discipline. Preferred Qualifications Doctoral degree (PhD, MD, DO, DVM, or equivalent) in a relevant discipline. Prior experience as a teaching assistant, lecturer, laboratory instructor, resident educator, fellow educator, tutor, or instructor. Experience in pathology, histology, histopathology, laboratory medicine, molecular diagnostics, or related biomedical fields. Familiarity with learning management systems (i.e. Brightspace/D2L, MedLearn, Canva, New Innovations, etc.). Familiarity with educational technologies (Brightspace/D2L, TopHat, Panopto, WeVideo/PlayPosit, Thinglink/VR, Kahoot!, Zoom, etc.) Familiarity with digital histology/pathology platforms (Leica/Aperio, DigitalScope, Philips/Grundium, Aiforia, PathPresenter, etc.) Interest in curriculum development, educational scholarship, and academic medicine. Rank Lecturer Tenure Information Career-Track (CT) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Dr Mark Nelson mnelson@pathology.arizona.edu Open Date 10/15/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV) and Cover Letter Special Instructions to Applicant Application: The online application should be completed in its entirety. Blank or missing information may be considered an incomplete submission. Cover Letter: Should clearly indicate how your skills and professional employment experience meet the Minimum and the Preferred qualifications (if applicable). Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27329?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Associate Dean, Graduate College Administration (T)",
      "description": "Associate Dean, Graduate College Administration (T) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Associate Dean, Graduate College Administration (T) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27329 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/15/2026 Back to Search Associate Dean, Graduate College Administration (T) Posting Number req27329 Department Graduate College Admin Department Website Link https://grad.arizona.edu Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Graduate College Associate Dean of Student Success provides collaborative, strategic leadership, playing a major role in planning, improving, and evaluating graduate student success. The successful candidate will work closely with the Dean to promote innovations in graduate education and student success through collaboration with directors of graduate studies, department heads/school directors, and other campus administrators (e.g., the Office Research and Partnerships) in order to enhance success for every graduate student. They will provide oversight for all student services and support throughout the graduate student lifecycle and supervise a team of staff and career-track faculty who conduct the day-to-day operational activities of the Graduate Center. In addition, the individual in this role will lead teams to submit grant submissions that support the success of graduate students. As part of this role, they will collaborate with campus leaders, university systems, and institutional offices on matters connected to graduate student success and support. This individual will report directly to the Vice Provost for Graduate Education and Dean of the Graduate College. The major duties and responsibilities are listed below, and other duties may be assigned. The University of Arizona Graduate College plays a central role in advancing graduate education, research excellence, and student success across the university. The Graduate College supports graduate programs by fostering an academic environment that prepares students for leadership in research, industry, public service, and academia. Through collaboration with colleges and departments campus-wide, the Graduate College promotes innovative scholarship, professional development, and access to graduate education while upholding the highest standards of academic integrity and mentorship. Committed to student-centered support and institutional impact, the Graduate College drives initiatives that strengthen graduate outcomes, research engagement, and the university’s mission as a leading public research institution. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Strategic Analysis and Management of Graduate Student Success Initiatives: Collaborates in the design of and advocates for initiatives that advance Graduate College strategic goals, including fostering innovative ideas in graduate education, increasing access to and support for students across the graduate student lifecycle, and stewarding resources and finances wisely. Provides leadership and strategic guidance on graduate student success policies, procedures, and best practices, including active participation in the development and implementation of goals, ensuring that strategies have aligned initiatives, implementation, and assessment plans. Serves as a resource for leadership on matters related to Graduate College workload policies and procedures, including working with team(s) to roll out new processes or process changes across campus. Directs initiatives to improve the pipeline of undergraduate students progressing to graduate school and collaborates on the design and implementation of graduate student recruitment and enrollment initiatives. Collaborates with Graduate College senior leadership on student grievance procedures and oversees cases related to graduate student support. Plans, directs, and reviews the activities and operations of the Graduate Center team to ensure achievement of established goals and objectives. Oversees the recruitment, programming, and outcomes of the University Fellows program. Develops long- and short-range goals to identify opportunities to improve operational functions and structures and strategically align student support services with overall goals of the University. Directs, manages, and evaluates the day-to-day operational activities and outcomes associated with student success initiatives, including future staffing requirements which may emerge and succession planning. In collaboration with the Graduate College management team, collects and analyzes student success data and its relationship to timely completion of degrees. Lead Research Initiatives on Graduate Student Success: Provides strategic leadership in the development, submission, and implementation of grant proposals focused on innovative concepts to improve graduate student success across the lifecycle. Leads the Graduate Center team in identifying emerging challenges, opportunities, and evidence-based practices related to graduate student academic progress, mentorship, well-being, professional development, degree completion, and career outcomes. Collaborates with the Office of Research and Partnerships and other university offices to strengthen grant development strategies and align with institutional priorities. Ensures that graduate students’ perspectives, including those of students from varied backgrounds and academic pathways, inform research priorities, program design, and institutional recommendations. Translate research findings and assessment results into actionable strategies, policies, programs, and resource-allocation recommendations for the Graduate College and academic units. Develop pilot initiatives based on research findings and oversee their implementation, refinement, and potential expansion across the university. Leads the Graduate Center team in disseminating findings through institutional reports, presentations, scholarly publications, professional conferences, grant reports, and communications to university leadership and campus partners. Cultivating Campus Partnerships: Designs, coordinates, and evaluates outreach efforts to increase utilization of Graduate Center programming. Collaborate with colleges and directors of graduate programs to enhance and expand the portfolio of student success programs offered, including strategic and innovative initiatives stemming from research projects. Collaborates with colleges and directors of graduate programs to resolve graduate student grievances. Collaborates with graduate programs to identify opportunities for program improvement and incorporate best practices to facilitate foundational support across the graduate student lifecycle. Collaborates with the Offices for Native American Initiatives and Hispanic Serving Institutions on initiatives to improve graduate student support. Minimum Qualifications Doctoral degree in a related field. The successful candidate must hold the rank of associate or full professor with tenure. The candidate must demonstrate at least 5 years of service to graduate education through contributions from the Graduate Council, departmental/school committees related to graduate education, graduate program leadership, and/or collaboration on external grants supporting graduate student success. Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated expertise in data analysis and interpretation, paired with excellent verbal and written communication skills. Demonstrated experience collaborating with faculty members, staff, students, and external partners. Demonstrated successful leadership of teams. Prior experience with revising and implementing academic policies. FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Fingerprint criminal background check (security sensitive due to job duties) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Kirsten Limesand Vice Provost/Dean, Graduate Education limesank@u.arizona.edu Open Date 10/15/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV) and Cover Letter Special Instructions to Applicant Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27272?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Professor (Tenure)",
      "description": "Associate/Full Professor (Tenure) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Associate/Full Professor (Tenure) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27272 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/15/2026 Back to Search Associate/Full Professor (Tenure) Posting Number req27272 Department James C Wyant Coll Optical Sci Department Website Link Wyant College of Optical Sciences Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Wyant College of Optical Sciences (OSC) at the University of Arizona invites applications from exceptional scientists and engineers for the William F. and Helen M. Reynolds Endowed Chair in Optical Sciences, a tenure/tenure-track faculty position in the general area of Optical Engineering. We envision hiring at the full or associate professor rank for this endowed chair position.  Applicants may also be considered for a non-chaired tenure-track assistant professor position. We encourage applications from researchers whose expertise and interests will complement or strengthen current efforts at the Wyant College of Optical Sciences and across the University of Arizona. The Wyant College of Optical Sciences seeks to create a supportive environment for all faculty, staff, and students. We welcome and encourage all qualified candidates to apply. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities The Wyant\nCollege of Optical Sciences at the University of Arizona is world-renowned for\nthe breadth and depth of its research and education programs. The college’s\nOptical Engineering program is one of four major research areas and the core of\nour undergraduate program with a broad scope of activity and interests. \nThese range from innovations in broad areas of optical engineering (e.g.\nprecision optical fabrication techniques, optical system design methods,\noptical metrology, opto-mechanical engineering), novel design of\nhigh-performance imaging and non-imaging systems, optical materials and\nmetamaterials, biomedical optics and instrumentation, augmented and virtual\nreality (AR/VR) technologies, optics for astronomy and space exploration,\ninfrared systems, hyperspectral imaging, remote sensing and lidar. The position\nis not limited to these areas and applicants in other fields of optical\nengineering are encouraged to apply. The Wyant\nCollege of Optical Sciences has also been fortunate to receive philanthropic\nsupport to create 14 Endowed Chairs in Optical Science positions in the\ncollege.  The William F. and Helen M. Reynolds Endowed Chair in Optical\nSciences is one of three remaining endowed chairs to be filled with an\ninaugural recipient. The\nsuccessful candidate will develop their own independent research program to\nbroaden the capabilities of the College but also find synergy with current\nWyant College of Optical Sciences research programs and benefit from the\nvibrant cross-disciplinary research culture at the University of Arizona. The\nOptical Engineering group enjoys particularly strong linkages with Astronomy,\nSpace Optical Systems, Engineering and Biomedical Engineering, and Department of Radiology and Imaging Sciences. Joint appointments in the various departments across multiple colleges\nare also possible. Experience in\nprivate-sector enterprises can be a valuable candidate asset. The Wyant College\nof Optical Sciences, and the Optical Engineering group in particular, enjoy\nstrong engagement with private-sector stakeholders addressing previously\nunknown challenges and intellectual opportunities encountered while advancing\ncutting edge technologies to the market. Duties and\nResponsibilities: Candidates\nfor this tenure/tenure-track position should demonstrate a commitment to\nexcellence both in research in their field and teaching at the undergraduate\nand graduate levels. Duties include: Cultivating a world-renowned\n     research program. Establishing a strong record of\n     student mentorship and teaching. Participating in both\n     undergraduate and graduate education. Participating in service and\n     outreach for the College. For\nprivate sector or government laboratory applicants, lack of a proven teaching\nor extramural research funding record could be addressed with other related\ncredentials that demonstrate strong communications skills and a demonstrated\nhistory of technical depth and leadership.  We seek an individual who is\nable to work with and attract various undergraduate and graduate students and\ncolleagues.  Development of novel courses pertaining to the candidate’s\narea of expertise, as well as exceptional teaching of our core curriculum, is\nexpected. Minimum Qualifications The applicant must hold a Ph.D.\n     (or an equivalent doctorate degree) in Optics, Physical Sciences,\n     Engineering or related fields. Candidates with all levels of expertise beyond\nthe Ph.D. may apply. Preferred Qualifications Experience and demonstrated leadership roles in post-doctorate research, and teaching and industrial experience in optics is desirable. Experience in private-sector enterprises can be a valuable candidate asset. Rank To be Determined Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Dr. Ron Driggers, rdriggers@arizona.edu Open Date 10/8/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Resume, Cover Letter, and Three Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Please upload the following documents with your application: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae which includes list of publications, and the names and contact information for three references as the final page Statement of Teaching Philosophy Statement of Research Interests If your background includes teaching experience, please submit teaching examples such as: statement or portfolio of teaching experience Documents 3-5 may be uploaded into the additional document sections. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27360?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor, School of Dance (Multiple Positions)",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor, School of Dance (Multiple Positions) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Adjunct Instructor, School of Dance (Multiple Positions) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27360 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Adjunct Instructor, School of Dance (Multiple Positions) Posting Number req27360 Department School of Dance Department Website Link https://dance.arizona.edu/ Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The University of Arizona School of Dance seeks Part-Time Adjunct Instructor(s) beginning January 13, 2027. As a nationally recognized dance program at a public research university, we welcome applicants with expertise across a broad spectrum of dance and movement practices. We are particularly seeking candidates with expertise in Pilates, with additional experience in one or more of the following areas welcomed: Jazz, Modern, Contemporary, Ballroom, Tap, Pointe, Choreography, yoga, and other related movement practices. The successful candidate will teach studio practice and/or lecture courses within their area(s) of expertise and may have opportunities to create performance or choreographic work. Candidates should demonstrate a commitment to student-centered teaching and contribute to the mission and academic community of the University of Arizona School of Dance. The School of Dance fosters a student-centered learning environment that encourages problem-solving, critical thinking, and conceptual analysis skills. We maintain a unique focus on performance, choreography, and pedagogy to prepare students for professional careers. The School of Dance is part of the Arizona Arts division, which serves as a gateway to visual and performing arts experiences, programs, and assets on the University of Arizona (U of A) campus. We seek a candidate who aligns with the core values of the university and has a commitment to learning for all with a devotion to the arts. We respectfully acknowledge the University of Arizona is on the land and territories of Indigenous peoples. Today, Arizona is home to 22 federally recognized tribes, with Tucson being home to the O’odham and the Yaqui. The University strives to build sustainable relationships with sovereign Native Nations and Indigenous communities through education offerings, partnerships, and community service. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Teach all\n     levels of studio courses, with primary consideration given to candidates\n     with expertise in Pilates. Additional areas\n     may include Jazz, Modern, Contemporary, Ballroom, Tap, Choreography,\n     Ballet/Pointe, and related dance and movement practices. Mentor      undergraduate and graduate students through studio instruction,\n     choreographic processes, and academic or professional development. Teach\n     courses within the candidate’s area(s) of\n     expertise, which may include: - Dance\n      Technique/Studio Courses, including Pilates, Jazz, Modern, Contemporary,\n      Ballroom, Tap, Choreography, and Ballet/Pointe. - Choreographic\n      Pedagogy and Mentoring - Improvisational\n      Forms - Music and\n      Dance Collaboration - Business/Careers\n      in Dance - Dance\n      History, including non-Western histories - General\n      Education and Academic Lecture Courses, in-person and/or online - Dance\n      Technology and Digital Media Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Demonstrated\n     ability to implement innovative pedagogies and effective classroom\n     strategies that foster meaningful and impactful learning experiences. Ability to\n     cultivate an engaged, collaborative, and supportive learning environment that\n     encourages personal growth and artistic development. Strong\n     pedagogical, organizational, communication, and interpersonal skills. Demonstrated\nexpertise and professional experience in Pilates and/or other relevant dance or\nmovement practices. Minimum Qualifications A Bachelor’s degree in Dance or related discipline or Equivalent Professional Dance Experience. Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching dance in an academic setting or intensive dance training environment. Master's degree in Dance or related discipline  or Equivalent Professional Dance Experience. Rank Instructor Tenure Information Adjunct (NTE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Part Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 4 - 19.6 Job FTE 0.10 - 0.49 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible No Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type Per Course Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 2 Target Hire Date Expected End Date 5/23/2027 Contact Information for Candidates Elizabeth George | egeorge@arizona.edu and Tamara Dyke-Compton | tdykecompton@arizona.edu Open Date 10/14/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and One Additional Document Special Instructions to Applicant Please submit your Curriculum Vitae (CV) and Cover Letter and one teaching video. The teaching video should show you working with advanced or intermediate dancers. Video should demonstrate ability to deliver: 1. An effective warm up. 2. Informed corrections and communication skills. 3. Movement exercises that are appropriate to the level. 4. A progression and adaptation of content that is made clear to students and evident to the review committee. Video may be edited to show examples of the four criteria above. Please include a URL link in your submission. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27277?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Physics (Quantum Condensed Matter) (T/TE)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Physics (Quantum Condensed Matter) (T/TE) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor, Physics (Quantum Condensed Matter) (T/TE) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27277 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor, Physics (Quantum Condensed Matter) (T/TE) Posting Number req27277 Department Physics Department Website Link https://physics.arizona.edu/ Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The University of Arizona (UA) Physics Department invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in theoretical condensed matter/quantum physics starting Fall 2027. The existing condensed matter group includes four theorists, and four experimentalists. Areas of interest of the search are broadly defined and may include, but are not limited to, quantum materials, quantum many-body physics, two-dimensional and low-dimensional systems, magnetism and spintronics, correlated and topological phenomena, nonequilibrium and ultrafast physics, quantum transport, and quantum information science. We are particularly interested in candidates whose theoretical research can establish strong connections with experimentalists within the university. The Department of Physics at the University of Arizona is home to a vibrant and internationally recognized research enterprise. Our faculty lead major scientific collaborations, mentor the next generation of researchers, and drive discoveries that expand our understanding of the universe while advancing fields such as technology and quantum science. Faculty and students pursue groundbreaking research across a broad range of exciting disciplines, contributing new knowledge that not only shapes the future of physics but also delivers meaningful benefits to society. If you are passionate about advancing scientific discovery through research, education, and collaboration, we encourage you to apply and join our team! Application reviews will begin on November 30, 2026. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities The\nsuccessful candidate will have a proven track record in innovative research in theoretical\ncondensed matter and/or quantum physics. In addition to establishing a leading\nresearch program, the successful candidate will also be expected to demonstrate\nexcellence in teaching and service in the Physics Department. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D.\nin Physics or a related field. Preferred Qualifications Postdoctoral experience in theoretical condensed matter/quantum physics or equivalent. Record of leadership in the field. Teaching/mentoring experience. Demonstrated record of innovation in condensed matter/quantum physics. Experience collaborating with experimental research groups. Rank Assistant Professor Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Professor John Schaibley, Search Committee Chair johnschaibley@arizona.edu Open Date 10/14/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Three Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Please upload the following documents with your application: Curriculum Vitae, including a list of publications. Cover Letter with a description of research focus and teaching experience (1 page max.) Research Statement with a description of significant research accomplishments and a plan for future research (4 pages max.) Teaching and mentoring statement (2 pages max.) Contact information for three reference writers. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27180?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Quantitative, Population, and Community Ecology (T/TE) (updated)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Quantitative, Population, and Community Ecology (T/TE) (updated) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor of Quantitative, Population, and Community Ecology (T/TE) (updated) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27180 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor of Quantitative, Population, and Community Ecology (T/TE) (updated) Posting Number req27180 Department Ecology & Evolutionary Biology Department Website Link https://eeb.arizona.edu Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights Please Note: This posting has been updated to include an application review date. The Department of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology at the University of Arizona in Tucson invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Quantitative, Population, and Community Ecology. We seek applicants who address fundamental questions in ecology, especially questions in population and community ecology, using quantitative approaches grounded in theory, experiments, observations, or field studies. We also welcome research that examines how biodiversity and evolutionary processes shape ecological dynamics. Relevant areas may include population dynamics, species interactions, community assembly, eco-evolutionary dynamics, ecoinformatics, spatial and temporal dynamics, ecological networks, phylogenetics, biodiversity change, and ecological responses to environmental change. We are particularly interested in scientists who develop or apply quantitative and computational methods to explain and predict ecological and eco-evolutionary dynamics to global change. Approaches may include mathematical and statistical modeling, machine learning, artificial intelligence, simulation, causal inference, ecological forecasting, biodiversity informatics, remote sensing, or integrating large, heterogeneous biological datasets. We welcome researchers with expertise in particular organisms or ecological systems, as well as those who integrate across approaches, systems, scales, or levels of biological organization. We encourage research programs that connect ecological, biodiversity, and evolutionary theory with data, quantify uncertainty, generate testable predictions, and leverage the University’s experimental, field, data-science, and computational resources. Founded in 1975, the Department of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology at the University of Arizona was among the first departments of its kind in the world, pioneering a model for the organization of biology that is now in use in many of the world’s leading universities. Our focus is broad, spanning levels of organization from molecular genetics and organismal function as they relate to evolution and ecology, from population, community and ecosystem ecology to biological diversity, phylogeny, and macroevolution. Our program’s unique personality comes from our faculty members who combine theory and empirical work and take an integrative approach to blending disciplines in their research and teaching. We are excited about building a collaborative and supportive environment for research, education, and service. Tucson is a fun, affordable, welcoming, and livable city (and is now a UNESCO World City of Gastronomy). We have great weather and are surrounded by spectacular scenery, including numerous national parks and wilderness areas. The Tucson area has a surprising range of habitats, amazing subtropical biodiversity, and is only a few hours’ drive from numerous tropical habitats in Mexico. The first review of applications will begin on November 16, 2026. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Establish and maintain a competitive research program. Teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels and contribute to mentoring and supporting student success. Participate in outreach activities and contribute to departmental, college and university service. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Ecology and Evolutionary Biology or a related discipline; the selected candidate must have a conferred Ph.D. upon hire. Research experience in quantitative methods in population and community ecology. Preferred Qualifications Experience developing an independent research program. Strong track record of peer-reviewed publications. Experience establishing, or demonstrated potential to establish a strong, high-visibility, high-quality, externally funded research program. Experience in establishing or demonstrated potential to lead a successful and productive research group. Rank Assistant Professor Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Brian Enquist, PhD Professor, Ecology and Evolutionary Biology benquist@arizona.edu Open Date 10/14/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Five Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Applications should include: A cover letter describing how the applicant’s research relates to the focus of this search. A current curriculum vitae (CV). A two-to three-page statement of research interests. A one-page statement of teaching philosophy. A one-page statement of service and outreach activities. Three reprints or preprints of the candidate’s publications (uploaded as publication samples). Contact information for three referees who can provide letters of recommendation. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27260?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Practice, Technical Directing",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Practice, Technical Directing | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor of Practice, Technical Directing Tucson, AZ, United States | req27260 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/12/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor of Practice, Technical Directing Posting Number req27260 Department Sch Theatre Film & Television Department Website Link https://tftv.arizona.edu/ Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The University of Arizona's School of Theatre, Film & Television (TFTV) invites applications for a full-time Assistant Professor of Practice in Technical Direction beginning August 2027. This Career-Track position plays a vital role in supporting our BA training in Live and Screened Performance, the Theatre Arts minor, and the Musical Theatre minor by integrating rigorous classroom instruction with hands-on production practice. TFTV is a vibrant, collaborative community of artists and scholars dedicated to storytelling across stage and screen. The School offers nationally respected training in theatre arts, film and television production, musical theatre, and live and screened performance. Students learn in an environment that emphasizes interdisciplinary collaboration, professional preparation, and creative research. With access to state-of-the-art production facilities, performance venues, and film production resources,TFTV prepares students to become innovative leaders in the performing and media arts industries. Our faculty are accomplished practitioners and scholars committed to mentorship, excellence, and fostering a culture of curiosity and experimentation. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Teaching & Mentorship: Teach undergraduate courses in technical theatre and production, including lecture, laboratory, and production-based courses in areas such as technical direction, scenic construction, stagecraft, drafting, and production management. Develop, assess, and maintain a rigorous, practice-based curriculum that integrates theoretical foundations with hands-on production experience and prepares students for professional careers in technical theatre and entertainment production. Mentor, supervise, and evaluate students serving in roles such as Assistant Technical Directors, scenic carpenters, and technical production assistants within the School's production program. Provide individualized mentorship that supports the development of technical, collaborative, and leadership skills necessary for work in professional theatre and related industries. Integrate experiential learning, critical thinking, and industry best practices into coursework and production activities in alignment with the Provost's “Success for Every Student” priorities. Cultivate an inclusive and pluralistic learning environment that promotes dignity, belonging, and empowerment for all students. Production Leadership: Serve as Technical Director for productions in the School's four-show subscription season, overseeing the realization of scenic designs from concept through strike. Manage the technical planning and execution of scenic production, including drafting interpretation, engineering solutions, budgeting, materials procurement, construction, installation, and technical integration. Collaborate closely with directors, designers, faculty, staff, and student production teams throughout the design and rehearsal process to ensure that technical solutions support artistic goals while maintaining feasibility, efficiency, and safety. Attend production meetings and technical rehearsals; advise on production feasibility, scheduling, and technical resource allocation. Model professional standards and best practices while guiding students through the full technical production process, from design development through construction, installation, performance, and strike. Maintain an active professional practice that contributes to a creative and scholarly profile. Shop & Facility Management: Supervise the daily operations of the scene shop and related production facilities, including scheduling, workflow coordination, and oversight of student crews working on scenic construction and installation. Maintain and enforce safe working practices in accordance with institutional policies and industry safety standards, including training students in safe equipment operation, rigging, and shop procedures. Oversee equipment maintenance, inventory management, and the acquisition of tools, materials, and shop supplies, ensuring that facilities support the instructional and production mission of the program. Manage scenic construction budgets in coordination with production management and departmental leadership, ensuring responsible and efficient use of resources. Season Planning & Production Collaboration: Participate in season planning and production selection discussions, providing early technical consultation regarding feasibility, budgeting, and scheduling considerations. Collaborate with design and production faculty during the design development process to evaluate technical requirements, materials, construction methods, and shop capacity. Contribute to the development of production calendars, build schedules, and technical timelines that support efficient production workflows and meaningful student participation. Support a collaborative production culture that integrates artistic goals with technical innovation, safety, and effective resource management. Collaboration & Service: Collaborate with faculty and the School Director to evaluate and advance undergraduate curriculum. Contribute to curriculum development, faculty governance, audition coordination, committee work, and administrative service. Participate in initiatives across the College of Fine Arts, Arizona Arts, and the broader university, including public engagement and community outreach. Knowledge, Skills, Abilities: Skilled in successful production leadership; Ability to maintain safe shop and production practices; Ability to maintain ongoing professional engagement in the field, including conferences, workshops, or professional organizations; Knowledge of and ability to apply collaborative production processes to effectively work with students, directors, designers, performers, and production staff. The successful candidate will demonstrate collaborative leadership, student-centered pedagogy, and a commitment to artistic and academic excellence. Minimum Qualifications BA or BFA in Technical Theater, Theater Design, or related field Professional experience as a technical director in a variety of production environments Preferred Qualifications MFA in Technical Theater, Theater Design and Technology, or a related field. Experience teaching at the University level. Experience supervising student crews, coordinating production teams, and managing theatrical production budgets. Rank Assistant Professor Tenure Information Career-Track (CT) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Professor Rick Wamer, rwamer@arizona.edu Open Date 10/12/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Three Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Please include the following with your application: CV Cover Letter Two letters of recommendation that speak to your teaching experience and effectiveness, work ethic and collegiality, the quality and impact of professional activity, and why you are a good match for the position. One document demonstrating your teaching effectiveness Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27296?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Philosophy (Consciousness) (T/TE)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Philosophy (Consciousness) (T/TE) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor, Philosophy (Consciousness) (T/TE) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27296 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/9/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor, Philosophy (Consciousness) (T/TE) Posting Number req27296 Department Philosophy Department Website Link https://philosophy.arizona.edu/ Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The Department of Philosophy at the University of Arizona invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position specializing in interdisciplinary consciousness research, with an anticipated start date of August 16, 2027. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in the philosophy of consciousness and related areas, including philosophy of mind; philosophy of cognitive science, neuroscience, biology, and/or artificial intelligence; philosophy of religion; and aesthetics. The standard teaching load is two courses per semester, with approximately half of the courses focused on the philosophy of consciousness. The Department of Philosophy is housed within the College of Social and Behavioral Sciences. The successful candidate will participate actively in the Center for Consciousness Studies and the Graduate Interdisciplinary Program in Cognitive Science. Additional information about the department is available at https://philosophy.arizona.edu/ . Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Teaching (40%): Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in consciousness studies and other\ncore areas of philosophy, with particular responsibility for courses in the\nConsciousness degree programs. Prepare and deliver course materials, assess\nstudent learning, and provide academic guidance and supervision to graduate\nstudents. Standard teaching load: two courses per semester. Research (40%) : Develop and maintain an active interdisciplinary scholarly\nresearch program in consciousness studies and related areas; conduct original\nresearch and disseminate findings through publications, presentations, and\nother appropriate academic activities. Service\n(20%): Participate in service to the Department of Philosophy, the College of\nSocial and Behavioral Sciences, the Center of Consciousness Studies, the\nGraduate Interdisciplinary Program in Cognitive Science, the University of\nArizona, and the philosophy profession. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Knowledge of philosophical approaches to consciousness and related interdisciplinary research. Ability to conduct and disseminate original scholarship. Ability to teach undergraduate and graduate courses and explain complex philosophical concepts. Ability to collaborate across disciplines and contribute to departmental, university, and professional service. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in\nPhilosophy or another field relevant to interdisciplinary consciousness\nresearch by the date of employment. Applicants whose Ph.D. is not in Philosophy must demonstrate\nresearch and teaching qualifications pertinent to a leading Ph.D. program in\nPhilosophy. All applicants must demonstrate evidence of a promising research\nagenda and a commitment to undergraduate and graduate teaching. Ability to teach in the philosophy of consciousness and\nrelated areas identified in the position highlights. Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching undergraduate and/or graduate students in philosophy of consciousness or related interdisciplinary areas. Experience mentoring students and participating in interdisciplinary research or academic programs. Rank Assistant Professor Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Academic Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Dennis Tamblyn Program Coordinator, Senior dtamblyn@arizona.edu Open Date 10/14/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Five Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Please submit the following with your application: CV Cover Letter Research Statement Teaching Dossier Writing Sample At least 3, and no more than 4 Letters of Reference. These letters can be submitted the following ways: Attach the letters as a single document and upload to the application. Email the letters directly to Dennis Tamblyn, dtamblyn@arizona.edu. Send the letters through Interfolio to Dennis Tamblyn, dtamblyn@arizona.edu. Application reviews will begin on November 7, 2026. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27244?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor, Metaphysics/Epistemology (Philosophy) (T/TE) (Updated)",
      "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor, Metaphysics/Epistemology (Philosophy) (T/TE) (Updated) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant or Associate Professor, Metaphysics/Epistemology (Philosophy) (T/TE) (Updated) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27244 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/9/2026 Back to Search Assistant or Associate Professor, Metaphysics/Epistemology (Philosophy) (T/TE) (Updated) Posting Number req27244 Department Philosophy Department Website Link https://philosophy.arizona.edu/ Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights Please Note: This posting has been updated to clarify the Documents Required under the 'Special Instructions to Applicant' field. The Department of Philosophy at the University of Arizona invites applications for a full-time faculty position in metaphysics or epistemology in the analytic tradition, broadly construed. The position may be filled at the rank of tenure-track Assistant Professor, or tenured Associate Professor, with an anticipated start date of August 1, 2027. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in metaphysics and other core areas of philosophy, including intermediate logic, and supervise graduate students. The standard teaching load is two courses per semester. The successful candidate will maintain an active research program and participate in departmental, college, university, and professional service. The Department of Philosophy is housed within the College of Social and Behavioral Sciences. Additional information is available at https://philosophy.arizona.edu/ Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; sick leave and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Teaching (40%): Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in metaphysics and other core areas of philosophy, including intermediate logic; prepare and deliver course materials, assess student learning, and provide academic guidance and supervision to graduate students. Standard teaching load: two courses per semester. Research (40%): Develop and maintain an active scholarly research program in metaphysics, epistemology, or related areas; conduct original research and disseminate findings through publications, presentations, and other appropriate academic activities. Service (20%): Participate in service to the Department of Philosophy, the College of Social and Behavioral Sciences, the University of Arizona, and the philosophy profession. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Advanced knowledge of metaphysics or epistemology in the analytic tradition Knowledge of core areas of philosophy, including intermediate logic. Ability to conduct and disseminate original scholarly research in philosophy. Ability to teach undergraduate and graduate courses effectively and communicate complex philosophical concepts. Ability to provide academic guidance and supervision to graduate students. Ability to contribute to departmental, college, university, and professional service activities. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in\nPhilosophy by the date of employment. Area of specialization in metaphysics or epistemology in the\nanalytic tradition. Preparedness to teach undergraduate and graduate courses in\nmetaphysics and other core areas of philosophy, including intermediate logic,\nand to supervise graduate students. Assistant Professor applicants must demonstrate evidence of\na promising research agenda and a commitment to undergraduate and graduate\nteaching. Associate Professor applicants must demonstrate an\nestablished record of research, teaching, and service that would merit tenure\nat the University of Arizona. Preferred Qualifications Rank To be Determined Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar TBD Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date 8/16/2027 Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Dennis Tamblyn Manager of Administration dtamblyn@arizona.edu Open Date 10/9/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and Four Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Please submit the following with your application: CV Cover Letter Research Statement Teaching Dossier Writing Sample At least 3, and no more than 4 Letters of Reference. These letters can be submitted the following ways: Attach the letters as a single document and upload to the application. Email the letters directly to Dennis Tamblyn, dtamblyn@arizona.edu. Send the letters through Interfolio to Dennis Tamblyn, dtamblyn@arizona.edu. Application reviews will begin on November 1, 2026. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27320?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Division Chief, Generalist - Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology",
      "description": "Division Chief, Generalist - Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Division Chief, Generalist - Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology Tucson, AZ, United States | req27320 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/9/2026 Back to Search Division Chief, Generalist - Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology Posting Number req27320 Department Obstetrics and Gynecology Department Website Link https://obgyn.arizona.edu/ Medical Sub-Speciality OB/GYN: General Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights The College of Medicine-Tucson at the University of Arizona invites applications for the position of Generalist Division Chief within the Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. This is an exceptional opportunity for a visionary and transformative individual to provide strategic leadership and direction for all planning, policy, operations, and oversight of the administration, clinical outreach expansion, educational, research, and quality assurance aspects of the Division within a matrixed academic healthcare environment. In addition to being a division chief, the candidate is expected to practice full-scope generalist obstetrics and gynecology across all age ranges. This includes normal and high-risk prenatal care, labor and delivery management, and inpatient and outpatient gynecologic surgical procedures. The candidate is also expected to participate in collaborative research. A focus on healthcare delivery to underserved populations, maternal health disparities, or related areas of health-services research is preferred. The ideal candidate will be an excellent clinician, enthusiastic educator, and innovative researcher, who can build upon excellence in existing clinical, teaching, and team-based research programs. They will recognize the importance of developing an engaged and integrated faculty and staff to build and nurture strong cross-functional and multidisciplinary teams. The role will also include participation in the recruitment and retention of Division faculty. Appointment rank is commensurate with experience. The successful candidate will meet the requirements of either a career track or tenure track associate or full professor faculty according to the College and University guidelines. If selected for a career-track appointment, the appointment will not be eligible for tenure. Visa sponsorship is not available for this positions. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities General Core Duties Provide strategic leadership and management of day-to-day operations. Set a clear vision and execute programs providing for short- and long-term growth, value, and service for the Division with a spirit of innovation. Maintain appropriate standards of care in balance with the multiple, tripartite mission of the University. Promote collaboration and consensus-building within and beyond the Division by collaborating with the Dean, Department Chair, BUMG management, staff, and other physicians with the goal to improve service and quality to meet institutional goals in research, teaching, advocacy, and scholarly activities. Lead an accomplished team of faculty and mentor trainees. Drive the development of clinical programs, especially outreach maternity care programs. Create accountability in the Division. Demonstrate transparency, a deep sense of professionalism, an unwavering ethical compass, and strong support for interdisciplinary and interprofessional collaboration and institutional growth. Serve as the role model to advance the Division's participation in basic, translational and/or clinical research, clinical service, research education, and other service activities. Represent the Division in the areas of financial performance, faculty career development, research, education, and clinical care, while achieving local, regional, and national visibility and prominence in the subspecialty. Represent the Department in interdisciplinary conferences, conferring with other services concerning issues related to general obstetrics and gynecology. Oversee the Division's faculty peer review. Directly report to the Department Chair. Clinical Core Duties Strategically recruit and retain talented faculty to support clinical growth, expand the Division's research footprint, improve the national reputation of the Division at the University of Arizona and Banner Health, and ensure excellence in the education and training of medical students, residents, and fellows. Oversee the administration and participate in the clinical aspects of the Division, including the inpatient service and the outpatient clinics. Provide direct patient care in the inpatient and outpatient settings as a licensed and fully credentialed provider as a member of the Banner University Medical Group. Oversight of the members of the Division of General Obstetrics and Gynecology ensuring adequate weekday and call coverage and the delivery of timely, high-quality clinical care. Liaise with other clinical and/or basic science departments to foster an integrated teaching environment for the College. Oversee faculty peer review. Research Core Duties Develop and execute a high-impact research agenda to advance the Division's active basic, translational, and clinical research, clinical service, research education, and other service activities. Foster, support, and expand research creativity and productivity within the Department, across the College, and with the College's key partners. Ensure that the scope and focus of research performed within the Division is consistent with the mission and strategic direction of the College and the University. Ensure that faculty minimum sponsored funding expectations are met. Education Core Duties Collaborate with Academic Affairs and other educational offices at the College to ensure the Department and Division faculty are advancing the mission of the medical student, graduate student, and graduate medical education teaching programs. Provide teaching instruction for the residency and medical student educational programs by preparing and administering lectures and reviewing cases. Ensure the development, implementation, supervision, and evaluation of undergraduate, graduate, and postgraduate education programs in the Division. Ensure the participation of Division members in the education mission, including clinical and didactic teaching as well as mentorship (career guidance and scholarly projects) with learners. Representation from each Division in Department education committees and learner recruitment is expected. Support continuing professional development/medical education for faculty and staff. Maintain program accreditation, ensuring that the service meets or exceeds local and national standards of outcome performance. Financial/Operational Core Duties Responsibility for ensuring the fiscal accountability and sustainability of the Division. Oversee the financial status of the Division, including annual budget development with Department administration, monitoring adherence to budget in all areas, including clinical and research income and expenditures. Ensure effective and efficient operation of the Division. Adjust expenditures and staffing as needed to address changes in resources. Oversee the effective utilization of divisional space. Knowledge. Skills. and Abilities: Expert understanding of full-scope women's healthcare across the life span. Demonstrated skill in complex obstetric and gynecologic surgery, including advanced laparoscopy. Demonstrated knowledge of healthcare administration and regulation, including hospital operations, credentialing, quality and safety, regulatory compliance, billing and coding. Demonstrated knowledge of the requirements of academic medicine including ACGME, UME, curriculum design, and research infrastructure. Thorough knowledge of national clinical consensus statements and guidelines. Demonstrated skill in strategic and financial management, including regional growth strategies, budgeting, and clinical operations. Demonstrated ability to mentor junior faculty in clinical care, research, and education. Demonstration of excellence in communication and cross-disciplinary collaboration. Skill in management, mentorship, and clinical care across diverse populations with dedication to eliminating maternal health disparities in underserved communities. Demonstration\nof skills in empathy, listening, resilience, and team building. Minimum Qualifications Qualified candidates must possess a Doctor of Medicine (MD), Doctor of Osteopathy (DO), or equivalent degree from an accredited institution. Must be Board Certified by the American Board of Obstetrics and Gynecology, and eligible for unrestricted medical licensure in the State of Arizona. The candidate must also be eligible for a University of Arizona appointment at the rank of Associate or full Professor. If the individual selected for employment does not currently hold a faculty title with the University of Arizona College of Medicine-Tucson, they will be required to obtain faculty status. This will include obtaining letters of recommendation and review by the appropriate College review committee. Preferred Qualifications Additional health profession degree (e.g., PhD, MPH, MS, etc.). Demonstrated leadership in teaching, clinical care, research, and/or educational enterprises in an academic department of medicine or equivalent environment. Demonstrated commitment to patient care, reflecting a service-oriented, team-based philosophy with hospital administration and health professional colleagues from all disciplines, departments, and specialties. Successful track record of recruitment and development of faculty, trainees, and students. A record of solid performance as a skilled manager with the ability to grow revenues, meet budgets, measure and improve productivity and outcomes, and undertake appropriate business strategies to achieve Division, unit, Department, and/or enterprise goals in an academic medical arena. Experience in the development and successful implementation of academic (educational, patient care, research) programs and services, including program review, assessment, and accreditation. Demonstrated outstanding leadership in major subspecialty division/section at a (NIH funded) department of OBGVN. Demonstrated excellence in scholarly activity. Demonstrated experience in fundraising and community outreach. Rank To be Determined Tenure Information To be Determined FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE, Commensurate with AAMC Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Gerry Flores, gflores007@arizona.edu Open Date 10/9/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV) and Cover Letter Special Instructions to Applicant Application: The online application should be completed in its entirety. Blank or missed information may be considered an incomplete submission. Cover Letter: Should clearly indicate how your skills and professional employment experience meet the Minimum and the Preferred qualifications (if applicable). Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/27162?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Cellular & Molecular Medicine (Tenure Track)",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Cellular & Molecular Medicine (Tenure Track) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Cellular & Molecular Medicine (Tenure Track) Tucson, AZ, United States | req27162 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/9/2026 Back to Search Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Cellular & Molecular Medicine (Tenure Track) Posting Number req27162 Department Cellular & Molecular Medicine Department Website Link https://cmm.arizona.edu/ Medical Sub-Speciality Location Tucson Campus Address Tucson, AZ USA Position Highlights With strong support from the State of Arizona and the College of Medicine-Tucson, the Department of Cellular and Molecular Medicine (CMM) at the University of Arizona (UA) in Tucson, AZ seeks applications for a tenure-track faculty member in the area of cancer biology at the rank of Assistant/Associate/Full Professor. The anticipated start date is summer/fall of 2025. Candidates with a Ph.D. or M.D./Ph.D. and expertise in cancer biology will be considered. The successful candidate will be expected to establish an innovative research program in cancer biology research, secure extramural funding, and provide high-level instruction to medical and/or graduate students. Areas of interest and expertise are broad and include: (1) cancer cell biology; (2) tumor microenvironment, including cancer mechanobiology; (3) imaging and/or computational approaches to cancer modeling, (4) cancer evolution (e.g., genomic instability, mechanisms of drug resistance, etc.), and (5) bioinformatics. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis and the positions will remain open until filled. CMM is renowned for its expertise in cardiovascular, cancer, and respiratory biology and disease research. Applicants bridging any of these disciplines are especially encouraged to apply. CMM is a fast-growing, highly interdisciplinary unit whose faculty make pivotal contributions to UA's mission of multidisciplinary and collaborative discovery through cross-cutting basic and applied/translational research. Approximately 30% of CMM primary faculty work within or are affiliated with The University of Arizona Cancer Center (UACC). UACC is a National Cancer Institute-designated Comprehensive Cancer Center at the forefront for expanded, high impact precision cancer medicine. UACC has strengths in basic mechanisms of carcinogenesis, tumor microenvironment, disparities, and cancer prevention. UACC is supported by a broad array of research activities including Data Science, Human Immune Monitoring, Cancer Imaging, Genomics, and Metabolomics, and a comprehensive biorepository which provides biospecimen collection, processing, and storage coupled with clinical and risk factor data collection for a variety of cancers. The successful candidate will have access to excellent laboratory space, access to outstanding core facilities, administrative support, and generous start-up funding. Visa sponsorship is not available for this positions. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Develop and maintain an established, well-funded, research program. Participate in the development and conduct of innovative and significant research. Play an active role in teaching and training undergraduate, graduate, medical students, and postdoctoral fellows. Participate in appropriate departmental events, including seminars, retreats, and meetings. Participate in College of Medicine events. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Strong organizational skills and keen attention to detail. Strong oral and written communication skills. Computer literacy. Strong interpersonal skills and ability to communicate effectively. Ability to multi-task effectively. Ability to work effectively independently and in a team environment. High level of integrity, ethics and professionalism. General understanding of University of Arizona’s mission, purpose, and goals. Minimum Qualifications PhD and/or MD degree. Strong publication record in high impact journals commensurate with experience. Active extramural research funding. Preferred Qualifications Extensive postdoctoral research experience. Rank To be Determined Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay DOE Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 3 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Greg Rogers | gcrogers@arizona.edu Open Date 10/9/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Resume, Cover Letter, and Three Additional Documents Special Instructions to Applicant Please submit the following FIVE (5) documents: Cover Letter: Outline qualifications and desire to participate in Cancer Bioengineering Curriculum Vitae: Provide details on track record of research, publication, funding, and service Statement of Research Interests: Outline prior and planned research program Statement of Teaching Interests: Describe experience and philosophy of teaching Contact Information for Three References Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
    "https://arizona.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/23491?c=arizona": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate or Full Professor (Tenure Track), Translational Neurosciences (Phoenix)",
      "description": "Assistant, Associate or Full Professor (Tenure Track), Translational Neurosciences (Phoenix) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant, Associate or Full Professor (Tenure Track), Translational Neurosciences (Phoenix) Phoenix, AZ, United States | req23491 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/8/2026 Back to Search Assistant, Associate or Full Professor (Tenure Track), Translational Neurosciences (Phoenix) Posting Number req23491 Department COM Phx Translational Neurosci Department Website Link College of Medicine-Phoenix Medical Sub-Speciality Neurosciences Location Greater Phoenix Area Address 475 E. 5th St., Phoenix, AZ 85004  USA Position Highlights The University of Arizona College of Medicine – Phoenix (College) invites applications for multiple tenure-track faculty positions in the Department of Translational Neurosciences (TNS). We are seeking candidates who apply innovative approaches to investigate the development, function, and disease of the central nervous system at all level of analyses. Appointments will be based on experience. While all neuroscience areas will be considered, candidates with interests in neural circuitry function, psychiatric disorders, substance abuse, neuronal injury, age-related or neurodevelopmental disorders, inter-systemic comorbidities, neuroimmunology, sensory neurobiology, multiomics, or structural biology are encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will fulfill the responsibilities of a faculty member in the Department of Translational Neurosciences, actively participate in teaching, research/scholarship and service/outreach, and must qualify for an appointment at the rank of assistant, associate or full professor and meet the requirements of a tenure track faculty according to the College and University guidelines. Visa sponsorship is available for this position. The University of Arizona College of Medicine – Phoenix anchors the 28-acre Phoenix Bioscience Core (PBC) campus in the heart of the Valley of the Sun. The College inspires and trains individuals to become exemplary physicians, scientists, and leaders. The PBC campus embodies the University’s priorities of engagement, partnership, innovation, and synergy, in its world-class academic and research initiatives, with clinical facilities throughout Greater Phoenix. Outstanding U of A benefits include health, dental, and vision insurance plans; life insurance and disability programs; paid vacation, sick leave, and holidays; U of A/ASU/NAU tuition reduction for the employee and qualified family members; retirement plans; access to U of A recreation and cultural activities; and more! The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Establish active independent research programs. Demonstrate excellence in research and other scholarly work. Participate in department’s educational mission. Participate in activities including outreach, collaborative research, teaching and other departmental and institutional initiatives and service. Knowledge, Skills, & Abilities: Ability to collaborate and work synergistically with colleagues at all levels. Have outstanding interpersonal communication and professionalism skills. Be engaged in cutting-edge neuroscience research using advanced tools and technologies. Minimum Qualifications PhD and/or MD in a related field from an accredited university, with post-doctoral training. Demonstrate funding record consistent with experience and rank sought Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated research experience inclusive of ability to attain grant funding for research programs and studies. Have a track record of published research findings in leading journals. Rank To be Determined Tenure Information Tenure Track (T/TE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Full Time Number of Hours Worked per Week 40 Job FTE 1.0 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible Yes - Full Benefits Rate of Pay AAMC Public Schools for Rank and Specialty Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 4 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates talent@arizona.edu Open Date 7/31/2025 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and One Additional Document Special Instructions to Applicant Application: The online application should be completed in its entirety. Blank or missed information may be considered an incomplete submission. CV and cover letter is required for to be considered for the position. One additional document will be: professional reference contacts. Please include at least three professional references at your current rank or above, their contact information and the nature of your work with them. Faculty selected for this teaching opportunity will be required to obtain faculty status within the appropriate academic department. This will include obtaining letters of recommendation and review by the appropriate College review committee. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
    },
